Lots. First is understanding that Nethack is a survival puzzle solving game,
not just a hack and slash. If you just hack and slash the RNG conspires to
gang up on you.

Next is understanding how to take advantage of game balance. The DevTeam
realizes that if you are all powerful and everything is wimpy, it gets boring.
So as you progress, the game gets harder. The objective is to stay ahead of
the curve so that you are better than your environment at the time. There are
lots of elements to this. I'll outline a few.

1) Armor class. You'll survive a lot longer if monsters can't hit you and you
can limit your damage. Work hard to get your armor class down. Negative is
the best.

2) Weight/Speed. The faster you are, the harder it is to kill you. Stay 
unburdened. Work on getting wands/potions/boots of speed. Bags of holding
are excellent and a good reason to explore Sokoban.

3) Resistances/intrinsics. Dying by poison sucks. Work on getting poison
resistance. Shrieker meat is good for this. Work on magic resistance (I often
end up spending a wish for it) and magic cancellation. reflection is excellent
and a good reason to explore Sokoban.

4) Hit Points. You will get hit. Hit points buffers the damage. More is better,
but levelling up to get them can spoil your game balance advantage. Work on 
getting hit points without gaining levels. Healing potions and praying with
less than 6 HP both will give you a bump without gaining a level. Be judicious
and smart about it. Here's my typical outline:

I) Start off with 14HP at XL1. My god will give a prayer bump as long as I'm
under 16HP. So I wait 300 turns for the prayer timeout to expire and corner a
newt or jackal in a corner. Let them hit me until I get to 5HP. Pray. My god
restores me to full HP and adds up to 5 more to max. Often the jump is up to
17 or 18HP. BTW if I only get 1HP up to 15, I rinse and repeat.

II) Now level up to XL2. This adds a few more. Sometimes at 20, which then
I'll rinse and repeat step #I to get another god bump. Sometimes it's
21 or over and I continue on.

III) If I have healing potions (Xhealing is better), I'll quaff one or two 
which adds 1-3 more to the max HP. This puts me in the mid 20's for HP.

Note I'm still at XL2 instead of XL4 or XL5 just to get extra HP. So it limits
the monster generation somewhat by gives that extra HP buffer.

5) Pets. They are your best friends in the early game. Treat them well and they
will save your ass time and time again. Pets are the biggest exploit of game
balance in the early game. First they can gain experience and HP without 
impacting the game. Second is that they can get all the way to XL9 and 72HP
in a short amount of time. Third is that they become formidable weapons with
speed and multiple attacks. Finally they are completely loyal to you as long
as you hang around them and make sure that they eat. Not to mention the fact
that they will ID cursed items for you and will steal from shops. They are
totally indispensible in the early game. I always buff my pets in the upper
dungeon. I won't kill anything until my pet has moved from little dog/cat
status to dog/housecat status (Use the C command to call the pet and it'll
tell you what size they currently are.) I generally won't enter the Mines until
they are a large dog/cat. Even better is that when you come across a bones
level with a left large dog/cat you can tame them with a fortune cookie or
some other treat.

6) Elbereth. Monsters respect it. You can preplan by finding something to
engrave it with. Try engraving with gems that you find. If it Engraves instead
of writes in the dust, then you can use it to your advantage. Even writing
Elbereth in the dust can help you in a tight spot.

7) Ranged weapons. You're hard to kill if something isn't beating on you.
Ranged weapons/spells can inflict a lot of damage without actually putting
you in harms way.

So let's put it all together and run a simple experiment.

1) Start a game. Buff the pet by letting it kill everything. Work on getting
it to large dog/cat status.

2) Work on AC by IDing and wearing any not cursed armor you find. Use your
pet to test cursed status. Make sure you have a hard hat. Try to get your
AC negative if you can. Wear armor even if you're playing a spellcaster.

3) Bump your HP using the method outlined above.

4) Now go into the mines (with your large pet) and engrave Elbereth on the 
stairs and just stand there. See how long it takes before you're killed.

You'll probably find that it won't happen. Monsters will flee from you and your
large pet will clear the level for you. Your AC and HP will protect against
ranged weapon attacks.

The objective is to swing game balance in your favor. I hope that some of these
techniques will help you in doing so.

